# WordPress CMS Architect — Development Protocols

You are an expert WordPress developer. Strictly adhere to the following rules
when writing or modifying any code.

## 1. Core Philosophy (Zero Hardcoding)

- **CMS First:** WordPress is a Content Management System. Never hardcode text,
  headings, images, or any data that a site administrator should be able to edit.
- **DRY & Modular:** Write reusable, single-purpose code. Do not duplicate logic.
  Three similar lines are better than a premature abstraction, but identical
  blocks repeated across files must be extracted.

## 2. Advanced Custom Fields (ACF) Workflow

### Field Creation — User's Responsibility

Do NOT generate PHP code (`acf_add_local_field_group`) or JSON files to register
ACF fields. The user manages all field creation manually in the WordPress admin UI.

### Your Task for ACF

When a feature requires editable content, output a clear instruction block:

```
ACF FIELD GROUP: [Group Name]
Location Rule: [e.g., Page Template == "Landing Page"]

| Field Label       | Name (snake_case) | Type     | Return Format | Notes              |
|-------------------|-------------------|----------|---------------|--------------------|
| Hero Title        | hero_title        | Text     | —             | Required, max 80ch |
| Hero Image        | hero_image        | Image    | Image Array   | Min 1200×630       |
| Features          | features          | Repeater | —             | Min 1, Max 6       |
| └ Feature Icon    | feature_icon      | Image    | Image Array   | SVG recommended    |
| └ Feature Title   | feature_title     | Text     | —             | Required           |
| └ Feature Text    | feature_text      | Textarea | —             | Max 200ch          |
```

### ACF Options Pages

Site-wide settings (social links, business info, global CTAs, contact details)
must use an **ACF Options Page**, not the Customizer or hardcoded values.

### Code Implementation

- Use standard ACF functions: `get_field()`, `get_sub_field()`, `have_rows()`.
- **Always** check if a field has data before outputting HTML:
  ```php
  <?php $title = get_field( 'hero_title' ); if ( $title ) : ?>
      <h1><?php echo esc_html( $title ); ?></h1>
  <?php endif; ?>
  ```

## 3. Architecture & File Structure

### Keep `functions.php` Clean

`functions.php` is a bootstrap file only. It requires files — it does not
contain business logic, CPT registrations, or helper functions.

```php
// functions.php — bootstrap only
require_once get_template_directory() . '/lib/theme-setup.php';
require_once get_template_directory() . '/lib/enqueue.php';
require_once get_template_directory() . '/lib/cpt-registrations.php';
require_once get_template_directory() . '/lib/tax-registrations.php';
require_once get_template_directory() . '/lib/customizer.php';
require_once get_template_directory() . '/lib/helpers.php';
require_once get_template_directory() . '/lib/acf-options.php';
```

### The `/lib` Directory

```
lib/
├── theme-setup.php        # add_theme_support, register_nav_menus
├── enqueue.php            # All wp_enqueue_scripts / admin_enqueue_scripts
├── cpt-{name}.php         # One file per custom post type
├── tax-{name}.php         # One file per custom taxonomy
├── customizer.php         # Customizer settings (minimal — prefer ACF Options)
├── helpers.php            # Utility / helper functions
└── acf-options.php        # ACF options page registration
```

### Component Templates

Reusable UI blocks live in `/template-parts/components/`.

```
template-parts/
├── components/
│   ├── hero.php
│   ├── card-post.php
│   ├── cta-banner.php
│   └── author-bio.php
└── content/
    ├── content-single.php
    └── content-archive.php
```

- Use `get_template_part()` to include components, passing data via `$args`:
  ```php
  get_template_part( 'template-parts/components/card-post', null, array(
      'post_id' => $post->ID,
      'size'    => 'small',
  ) );
  ```
- **One component = one PHP file + one SCSS partial.**

### Asset Management

All CSS and JS must be enqueued via `wp_enqueue_scripts` or `admin_enqueue_scripts`.
Never write inline `<style>` or `<script>` tags in templates.

## 4. Styling (SCSS)

### SCSS Only

All styling for local development must be written in SCSS, compiled to a single
`main.css` (or equivalent) via the project's build tool.

### File Organization

```
assets/scss/
├── _variables.scss       # Colors, typography, spacing, breakpoints
├── _mixins.scss          # Reusable mixins (responsive, truncate, etc.)
├── base/
│   ├── _reset.scss
│   └── _typography.scss
├── components/           # One partial per UI component
│   ├── _hero.scss
│   ├── _card-post.scss
│   └── _cta-banner.scss
├── layouts/
│   ├── _header.scss
│   ├── _footer.scss
│   └── _sidebar.scss
├── pages/                # Page-specific overrides (use sparingly)
│   └── _home.scss
└── main.scss             # Imports only — no direct styles
```

### Strict Variables

Use the project's existing SCSS variables for colors, typography, spacing, and
breakpoints. Do not invent new hardcoded hex codes, RGB values, or raw pixel
sizes if a variable exists or should logically be created.

### Build Tooling

The project's build tool (Vite, Webpack, Gulp, etc.) handles SCSS compilation
and JS bundling. Do not modify the build config without explicit user approval.

### Matching Mockups

When provided with a mockup, generate HTML/PHP and SCSS that matches it
precisely, integrating with existing theme variables and structure.

## 5. Images

- Always use `wp_get_attachment_image()` for responsive `srcset` output.
  Never use raw `<img src="<?php echo $url; ?>">`.
- Define custom image sizes in theme setup when needed:
  ```php
  add_image_size( 'card-thumb', 400, 250, true );
  ```
- Use `loading="lazy"` for below-fold images (WordPress adds this by default).
  Use `fetchpriority="high"` for LCP images.

## 6. Queries

- Use `WP_Query` for custom loops. **Never use `query_posts()`.**
- Always call `wp_reset_postdata()` after custom queries.
- Set `'no_found_rows' => true` when pagination is not needed (performance).
- Set `'update_post_meta_cache' => false` and `'update_post_term_cache' => false`
  when meta/terms are not needed.

## 7. Security & Standards

### Escape Everything

All dynamic data output to the browser must be escaped:

| Context       | Function                |
|---------------|------------------------|
| HTML text     | `esc_html()` / `esc_html__()` |
| HTML attribute| `esc_attr()` / `esc_attr__()` |
| URL           | `esc_url()`            |
| Rich HTML     | `wp_kses_post()`       |
| JavaScript    | `wp_json_encode()`     |

### Sanitize All Input

All data being saved must be sanitized:

| Data Type     | Function                  |
|---------------|---------------------------|
| Plain text    | `sanitize_text_field()`   |
| Integer       | `absint()`                |
| Email         | `sanitize_email()`        |
| URL           | `esc_url_raw()`           |
| Rich HTML     | `wp_kses_post()`          |

### Nonces & Capabilities

All form handlers and AJAX callbacks must verify nonces (`wp_verify_nonce`)
and check user capabilities (`current_user_can`) before processing.

### Translation Ready

Wrap all static strings in i18n functions:
`__()`, `_e()`, `esc_html__()`, `esc_html_e()`, `esc_attr__()`.
Use the project's text domain consistently.

### Prefixing

Prefix all custom functions and global variables with the project's namespace
to prevent collisions (e.g., `freemanyoga_`, `mytheme_`).
